# ScenarioLab Technical Architecture and Runway Integration Plan

Author: **Manus AI**  
Date: May 8, 2026  
Purpose: **Technical handoff document for Claude, engineering research, and hackathon build planning**

## 1. Architecture summary

**ScenarioLab** should be implemented as a web application with a structured scenario engine, an orchestration backend, a Runway media adapter, an LLM-based evaluator constrained by expert-designed rubrics, and a privacy-first user profile and session store. Runway should be responsible for the product’s **embodied and cinematic layer**: real-time role-play characters, scenario visuals, replay clips, narrated summaries, and generated alternatives. The application itself should own the science-sensitive layer: scenario templates, rubrics, user preferences, safety rules, consent, progress tracking, and audit logs.

The architecture should deliberately avoid making Runway the clinical, behavioral, or evaluation engine. This separation is what makes ScenarioLab feasible for a hackathon and defensible as a longer-term product. Runway’s API and models support media creation and orchestration, while the app’s backend coordinates tasks, polls asynchronous media jobs, applies safety policy, and stores only the minimum data required for useful practice.[^1] [^2]

> **Technical thesis:** ScenarioLab is an agentic media system, not a single model call. The agent interprets a user goal, selects a curated scenario, launches an embodied interaction, evaluates the response against a constrained rubric, generates a replay artifact through Runway, and updates the user’s private progress state.

## 2. Recommended hackathon stack

For a hackathon, the stack should be optimized for fast iteration, clear demo quality, and safe handling of API keys. A full production stack can be more elaborate, but the MVP should not overbuild authentication, enterprise permissions, or longitudinal analytics.

| Layer | Recommended MVP choice | Why this choice works |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Frontend** | Next.js or Vite React + TypeScript + Tailwind | Fast interactive UI for scenario selection, role-play, replay, and progress cards. |
| **Backend API** | Node.js/Express, Next.js API routes, or FastAPI | Keeps Runway and LLM API keys server-side; coordinates async jobs. |
| **Database** | Postgres or SQLite for hackathon | Stores scenario templates, user preferences, sessions, rubric outputs, and media job state. |
| **Object storage** | S3-compatible storage or local files for demo | Stores generated media artifacts, reference images, and replay outputs. |
| **Runway integration** | Server-side Runway SDK/API wrapper | Creates and polls media tasks; isolates API details behind a `RunwayMediaService`. |
| **LLM evaluator** | Gemini/OpenAI-compatible model behind rubric constraints | Scores user response and drafts feedback; should not operate unconstrained. |
| **Realtime** | WebSocket/SSE for task updates | Lets frontend show “generating replay” and role-play state changes. |
| **Safety layer** | Rules engine + LLM classifier fallback | Blocks crisis, abuse, self-harm, sexual content, medical claims, and unsafe escalation. |

## 3. High-level system architecture

ScenarioLab should be designed around clear boundaries. The frontend never calls Runway directly because media API keys must remain private. The backend receives user actions, looks up structured templates, calls an evaluator, calls Runway through a media adapter, and streams job status to the frontend.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
  U[User] --> FE[Web App]
  FE --> API[ScenarioLab Backend API]
  API --> Auth[Consent and Role Permissions]
  API --> Scenario[Scenario and Rubric Engine]
  API --> Safety[Safety and Policy Engine]
  API --> Eval[LLM Evaluator]
  API --> Runway[Runway Media Adapter]
  API --> DB[(Database)]
  API --> Store[(Media Storage)]
  Runway --> RAPI[Runway API]
  RAPI --> Runway
  Runway --> Store
  API --> Events[WebSocket or SSE Status Events]
  Events --> FE
```

This architecture supports both a simple hackathon version and a future production version. For the hackathon, authentication can be lightweight, storage can be simple, and scenario data can be JSON files. For a real product, user consent, role-based access, deletion, encryption, auditability, and human review workflows need to be treated as core infrastructure.

## 4. Core services and responsibilities

The core technical design should separate **scenario control**, **agent orchestration**, **media generation**, **evaluation**, and **safety**. If these responsibilities are collapsed into one prompt, the product becomes hard to debug and unsafe to scale.

| Service | Responsibility | Key inputs | Key outputs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Scenario Service** | Loads curated scenario templates, difficulty levels, character profiles, and target skills. | `scenario_id`, `user_profile`, `difficulty`. | `ScenarioSessionPlan`. |
| **Profile Service** | Stores user preferences, accessibility settings, sensory intensity, and retention choices. | Onboarding form, session controls. | `UserProfile`, consent flags. |
| **Policy Engine** | Applies hard safety rules before and after model calls. | User text, scenario metadata, role permissions. | Allow, warn, refuse, or escalate. |
| **Conversation Orchestrator** | Manages turn-by-turn role-play and tool events. | Transcript, character state, scenario plan. | Next prompt, tool call, checkpoint. |
| **Rubric Evaluator** | Scores user response against narrow rubric and drafts feedback. | User response, target skill, rubric, preferences. | `EvaluationResult`. |
| **Replay Director** | Converts a session into a storyboard and Runway prompts. | Transcript excerpt, feedback, scenario visuals. | `ReplayStoryboard`, media prompts. |
| **Runway Media Service** | Calls Runway for character, image, video, audio, and replay generation. | Prompt text, input media, ratio, duration, model. | Media task ID, status, output URL. |
| **Progress Service** | Stores session summary, retry deltas, and social-validity ratings. | Evaluation result, retry data, user rating. | Progress card, next recommendation. |
| **Audit Service** | Stores scenario version, rubric version, prompts, model outputs, and policy decisions. | Service events. | Debug and review trail. |

## 5. Runway API integration strategy

Runway should be integrated as a **media capability provider** behind a server-side adapter. ScenarioLab should not scatter Runway calls across UI components. Instead, all media-related operations should pass through a small set of service methods. This makes it easy to change models, add retries, track task status, and mock Runway during development.

The Runway documentation indicates that model tasks are asynchronous: the application creates a task, then retrieves task status and output through the task lifecycle. The docs also show SDK-style usage where image-to-video calls include fields such as `model`, `promptImage`, `promptText`, `ratio`, and `duration`.[^2] Runway’s documentation and model guide describe capabilities across video generation, image generation, audio, and real-time characters, which map naturally to ScenarioLab’s practice partner and replay features.[^1] [^3]

| ScenarioLab feature | Runway capability | Integration pattern | MVP priority |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Embodied practice partner** | Runway Characters / real-time avatars | Character session receives role, opening line, tone, and tool-calling instructions. | P0 if access is available; otherwise use pre-generated avatar video plus text chat. |
| **Scene card** | Image generation | Generate visual scene thumbnail for workplace, classroom, interview, or clinic. | P1. |
| **Replay Lens** | Text/image-to-video generation | Convert transcript into storyboard, create prompt, call video generation, poll task, display result. | P0. |
| **Alternative response clip** | Text/image-to-video or video-to-video | Generate short “better response” clip based on evaluated feedback. | P1. |
| **Narrated reflection** | Audio / voice generation if available | Generate voiceover for replay and progress card. | P2. |
| **Localization and accessibility** | Dubbing/captions outside or alongside Runway | Add captions and text transcript; voice optional. | P1 captions; P2 dubbing. |

## 6. Runway media adapter design

The `RunwayMediaService` should expose product-level methods instead of raw model calls. This lets the rest of the application ask for a “Replay Lens” without knowing the exact model, parameters, or polling behavior.

```ts
interface RunwayMediaService {
  createSceneCard(input: CreateSceneCardInput): Promise<MediaJob>;
  createReplayLens(input: CreateReplayLensInput): Promise<MediaJob>;
  createAlternativeResponseClip(input: AlternativeResponseInput): Promise<MediaJob>;
  getMediaJobStatus(jobId: string): Promise<MediaJobStatus>;
  cancelMediaJob(jobId: string): Promise<void>;
}

interface CreateReplayLensInput {
  sessionId: string;
  scenarioId: string;
  storyboard: ReplayStoryboard;
  referenceImageUrl?: string;
  durationSeconds: 5 | 10;
  ratio: "1280:720" | "720:1280" | "960:960";
  safetyTags: string[];
}
```

For the hackathon, `createReplayLens` can generate one short clip from a structured prompt and reference image. In production, it should generate multiple segments: the moment, the likely interpretation, the alternative response, and the recap. The MVP can display the generated clip alongside captions and a transcript summary.

## 7. Agent loop design

ScenarioLab’s agent should be a **stateful orchestrator** that chains structured decisions. It should not be a single chatbot prompt. Each scenario session should move through defined states, and the agent should be able to call tools such as `evaluate_response`, `generate_replay`, `lower_intensity`, `offer_hint`, and `end_session`.

| State | Description | Tools available |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SETUP` | Load user preferences, retention settings, and scenario. | `load_profile`, `load_scenario`, `apply_policy`. |
| `PREVIEW` | Explain target skill, difficulty, and opt-out controls. | `show_instruction`, `adjust_intensity`. |
| `ROLEPLAY_OPENING` | Character delivers opening line. | `start_character_session`, `log_turn`. |
| `CUE_CHECK` | User identifies emotional or situational cue. | `score_cue`, `offer_hint`. |
| `USER_RESPONSE` | User responds to character. | `log_turn`, `apply_policy`. |
| `EVALUATION` | App evaluates response against rubric. | `evaluate_response`, `draft_feedback`. |
| `REPLAY_GENERATION` | App generates Replay Lens storyboard and Runway video. | `create_storyboard`, `create_replay_lens`, `poll_media_job`. |
| `RETRY` | User practices again with one adjustment. | `restart_roleplay`, `compare_attempts`. |
| `REFLECTION` | User rates usefulness, realism, respect, and intensity. | `save_progress`, `save_social_validity`. |
| `CLOSE` | User deletes, saves, or exports summary. | `delete_data`, `export_summary`. |

## 8. Detailed session flow

The session flow should be optimized for clarity and safety. The user should always know what is being practiced, why the system is giving feedback, and what data is being stored. This matters because autism-support AI can easily become opaque or surveillance-like if consent is not designed upfront.[^4]

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  participant User
  participant Frontend
  participant Backend
  participant Scenario as Scenario Service
  participant Safety as Policy Engine
  participant Eval as Rubric Evaluator
  participant Runway as Runway Media Service
  participant DB as Database

  User->>Frontend: Select goal and preferences
  Frontend->>Backend: POST /sessions/start
  Backend->>Scenario: Load scenario + rubric
  Backend->>Safety: Validate scenario and preferences
  Backend->>DB: Create session
  Backend-->>Frontend: Scenario preview
  User->>Frontend: Begin role-play
  Frontend->>Backend: POST /sessions/{id}/turns
  Backend->>Safety: Check user input
  Backend->>DB: Store structured turn metadata
  Backend-->>Frontend: Character response / next prompt
  User->>Frontend: Submit response
  Frontend->>Backend: POST /sessions/{id}/evaluate
  Backend->>Eval: Evaluate against rubric
  Eval-->>Backend: Scores + one feedback point
  Backend->>DB: Save evaluation
  Backend->>Runway: Create Replay Lens job
  Runway-->>Backend: Media job id
  Backend-->>Frontend: Job started
  Runway-->>Backend: Job complete + output URL
  Backend->>DB: Save media artifact
  Backend-->>Frontend: Display replay + feedback
  User->>Frontend: Retry or finish
```

## 9. Data model

The data model should reflect three categories of data: durable user preferences, scenario and rubric definitions, and ephemeral session artifacts. The system should avoid storing raw audio/video by default. If transcripts are stored, the user should know and be able to delete them.

### 9.1 Core tables

| Table | Purpose | Key fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `users` | Account identity. | `id`, `email`, `created_at`, `role`. |
| `user_profiles` | Communication and accessibility preferences. | `user_id`, `modality`, `feedback_style`, `intensity`, `topics_to_avoid`, `retention_policy`. |
| `scenario_templates` | Curated scenario definitions. | `id`, `domain`, `target_skill`, `difficulty`, `setting`, `opening_line`, `hidden_state`, `version`. |
| `rubrics` | Structured scoring definitions. | `id`, `target_skill`, `dimensions`, `examples`, `version`, `review_status`. |
| `sessions` | Practice session metadata. | `id`, `user_id`, `scenario_id`, `status`, `started_at`, `ended_at`, `retention_mode`. |
| `turns` | Role-play turn metadata. | `id`, `session_id`, `speaker`, `text_redacted`, `timestamp`, `policy_flags`. |
| `evaluations` | Rubric outputs. | `id`, `session_id`, `rubric_id`, `scores_json`, `feedback`, `confidence`, `evaluator_model`. |
| `media_jobs` | Runway task tracking. | `id`, `session_id`, `provider`, `provider_task_id`, `status`, `output_url`, `prompt_hash`. |
| `social_validity` | User feedback on scenario quality. | `session_id`, `useful`, `realistic`, `respectful`, `intensity_ok`, `free_text`. |
| `audit_logs` | Debug and safety review trail. | `id`, `actor`, `event_type`, `resource_id`, `metadata_json`, `created_at`. |

